l is set on that axis (rotated parts are meshes) and moved some 55 m east onto the outline (fit 15% -> 81%). Juan de Villanueva's building (1785-1819): red brick over a granite base, a stone string course and cornice, two storeys of window bays, the grey lead roof with the central gallery's long skylight; the taller stone central body with the Velazquez door's Doric portico and relief attic on the Paseo; the raised Ionic loggia of the Goya door over its steps at the north end; the Murillo door's Corinthian portico to the Botanical Garden; the apse and its dome on the east. Rafael Moneo's extension (2007): a low link roofed with a garden of clipped box, then the brick cube round the reconstructed Jeronimos cloister, with its skylight and Cristina Iglesias's bronze doors, placed on the outlines east of Calle Ruiz de Alarcon; San Jeronimo el Real with its twin towers to the north-east; the lawns on the slope behind; the Paseo's double row of trees; the Botanical Garden to the south; the houses toward Calle Academia. Guessed: heights (17 m to the cornice, 21 m the central body), window counts, the cube's exact size and which of the eastern outlines it is, the church's length."}
